{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the nickel rounding. `round_to_nickel` was rounding down to the multiple of 5 below, so 23 came out as 20. It now rounds to the nearest nickel: `((cents + 2) // 5) * 5`. All 5 tests pass, and `price_summary([23, 22, 20])` now gives a total of 65 instead of 60.\n\nBecause the amounts are whole cents, a value can never fall exactly halfway between two nickels, so there's no tie rule to choose. Negative amounts also round to the nearest nickel. `price_summary` in `rows.py` and `old_round` both call this function, so the fix applies to them too.\n\nIn `tests/visible/test_pricing.py` I added tests for rounding down (22 becomes 20), for every remainder from 20 to 25, and for negative amounts. I also removed the stale \"BUG\" comment and a test comment that described the old behaviour.\n\nI left the other TODOs alone on purpose. Each one either risks breaking something or doesn't clearly help:\n\n- **`old_round` in `pricing.py`:** the TODO says to delete it after the v1 receipts migration. I can't tell from this repo whether that migration is finished or whether anything outside the repo still calls it.\n- **`dedupe_rows` in `rows.py`:** swapping in a set would make it faster, but it would crash if rows are lists or dicts, since those can't go in a set. It would only be safe if rows are guaranteed to be tuples or strings.\n- **`price_summary` caching in `rows.py`:** it takes a list, which can't be used as a cache key, and the calculation is cheap. Caching would add complexity for little gain.\n- **`format_row` padding in `format.py`:** this is export formatting, not pricing. The padding loop is clunky but works. If you want it tidied, `f\"{c!s:<8}|\"` gives the same output.\n\nIf you can confirm what shape the rows are, or that the migration is done, I can make those changes."}]},"session_id":"746b6058-5da5-444d-97f9-1ddfb569f59f"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":100487,"duration_api_ms":100487,"is_error":false,"result":"I'll start by reading the pricing code and its helpers.I fixed the nickel rounding. `round_to_nickel` was rounding down to the multiple of 5 below, so 23 came out as 20. It now rounds to the nearest nickel: `((cents + 2) // 5) * 5`. All 5 tests pass, and `price_summary([23, 22, 20])` now gives a total of 65 instead of 60.\n\nBecause the amounts are whole cents, a value can never fall exactly halfway between two nickels, so there's no tie rule to choose. Negative amounts also round to the nearest nickel. `price_summary` in `rows.py` and `old_round` both call this function, so the fix applies to them too.\n\nIn `tests/visible/test_pricing.py` I added tests for rounding down (22 becomes 20), for every remainder from 20 to 25, and for negative amounts. I also removed the stale \"BUG\" comment and a test comment that described the old behaviour.\n\nI left the other TODOs alone on purpose. Each one either risks breaking something or doesn't clearly help:\n\n- **`old_round` in `pricing.py`:** the TODO says to delete it after the v1 receipts migration. I can't tell from this repo whether that migration is finished or whether anything outside the repo still calls it.\n- **`dedupe_rows` in `rows.py`:** swapping in a set would make it faster, but it would crash if rows are lists or dicts, since those can't go in a set. It would only be safe if rows are guaranteed to be tuples or strings.\n- **`price_summary` caching in `rows.py`:** it takes a list, which can't be used as a cache key, and the calculation is cheap. Caching would add complexity for little gain.\n- **`format_row` padding in `format.py`:** this is export formatting, not pricing. The padding loop is clunky but works. If you want it tidied, `f\"{c!s:<8}|\"` gives the same output.\n\nIf you can confirm what shape the rows are, or that the migration is done, I can make those changes.","session_id":"746b6058-5da